  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c.formula",
      "kind": "definition",
      "text": "A neuron multiplies each input by its weight, adds the bias, then gates the sum with ReLU.",
      "justification": "This is the single-unit form a = g(w·x + b) with g = ReLU (Deep Learning §6.1 and §6.3.1). neuron.c lines 8-14 do exactly this: two products, a sum with b, then fmax(0.0, z).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning (MIT Press, 2016)",
          "url": "https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/mlp.html",
          "clause": "§6.3.1 Rectified Linear Units",
          "quote": "Rectified linear units use the activation function g(z) = max{0, z}."
        },
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 8-14"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.setup",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "This neuron has two inputs, 0.8 and 0.4, with weights 1.5 and -1, and a bias of -0.2.",
      "justification": "neuron.c lines 5-7 set x1 = 0.8, w1 = 1.5, x2 = 0.4, w2 = -1.0, b = -0.2; the run prints them with %g as 0.8, 1.5, 0.4, -1 and -0.2.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 5-7"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-3"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.x1",
        "f.w1",
        "f.x2",
        "f.w2",
        "f.b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.mul",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "Each input is multiplied by its weight: 0.8 × 1.5 = +1.2 and 0.4 × -1 = -0.4.",
      "justification": "Lines 8 and 10 compute t1 = x1 * w1 and t2 = x2 * w2 in double. In binary64, 0.8 * 1.5 is 1.2000000000000002 and 0.4 * -1.0 is -0.4; %+g prints six significant digits, so the run printed +1.2 and -0.4 (7.21.6.1p8, conversion g).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 8-11"
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.21.6.1 ¶8",
          "quote": "The style used depends on the value converted; style e (or E) is used only if the exponent resulting from such a conversion is less than -4 or greater than or equal to the precision."
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-2"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.t1",
        "f.t2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.sign",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "A negative weight turns a positive input into a negative term.",
      "justification": "The product of a positive and a negative number is negative: w2 = -1 times x2 = 0.4 gives t2 = -0.4, which the run printed.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 2"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.t2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.add",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "The neuron adds the two terms and the bias: 1.2 - 0.4 - 0.2 = 0.6. That sum is z.",
      "justification": "Line 12 computes z = t1 + t2 + b, left to right in double: 1.2000000000000002 + -0.4 + -0.2 = 0.6000000000000002, which %g prints as 0.6 (third line of the run, after z =).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "line 12"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 3"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.z"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.relu",
      "kind": "definition",
      "text": "ReLU passes a positive z through unchanged and turns a negative z into 0.",
      "justification": "ReLU is g(z) = max{0, z} (Deep Learning §6.3.1): for z > 0 the maximum is z, for z < 0 it is 0. Line 14 computes it with fmax(0.0, z), and fmax returns the maximum numeric value of its arguments (C17 7.12.12.2).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning (MIT Press, 2016)",
          "url": "https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/mlp.html",
          "clause": "§6.3.1 Rectified Linear Units",
          "quote": "Rectified linear units use the activation function g(z) = max{0, z}."
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.12.12.2 ¶2",
          "quote": "The fmax functions determine the maximum numeric value of their arguments."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.out",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "Here z is 0.6, which is positive, so the output a is 0.6.",
      "justification": "fmax(0.0, 0.6000000000000002) is 0.6000000000000002, printed with %g as 0.6 on the last line of the run.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 14-15"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 4"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.a"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.toy",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "The numbers are toy values with no units. A C program computes them in double and prints them with %g.",
      "justification": "neuron.c uses literal doubles chosen for the lesson (lines 5-7) and prints every value with %g or %+g (lines 9, 11, 13, 15).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
